            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Invention of the Transistor</strong><br>\nIn 1947, along with John Bardeen and Walter Brattain, Shockley invented the point-contact transistor, which revolutionized electronic circuits. The transistor replaced the bulky and less efficient vacuum tubes, paving the way for miniaturized electronics and leading to the digital age.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physics</strong><br>\nThe groundbreaking work on the transistor earned Shockley, Bardeen, and Brattain the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1956. Their invention was acknowledged as one of the most important scientific developments of the 20th century. The prize recognized their contribution to the field of semiconductors and electronics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Silicon Valley Pioneer</strong><br>\nShockley's success with the transistor led him to establish his own semiconductor company in Mountain View, California, in 1956. This area would later become known as Silicon Valley, in part because of Shockley's role in developing the semiconductor industry in this region.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
